## Proof

**Yes, $\mu_1 = \mu_2$.**

Since $\mu_2 \ge \mu_1 \ge 0$, the difference $\nu := \mu_2 - \mu_1$ is a well-defined positive Radon measure, i.e., $\nu \ge 0$.

For any positive (Radon) measure $\mu$, the total variation norm coincides with the total mass:
$$\|\mu\| = |\mu|(\Omega) = \mu(\Omega),$$
since $|\mu| = \mu$ when $\mu \ge 0$.

Applying this to $\mu_1 \ge 0$ and $\mu_2 \ge 0$ (the latter follows from $\mu_2 \ge \mu_1 \ge 0$), and using $\mu_2 = \mu_1 + \nu$:

$$\|\mu_2\| = \mu_2(\Omega) = \mu_1(\Omega) + \nu(\Omega) = \|\mu_1\| + \nu(\Omega).$$

The hypothesis $\|\mu_2\| = \|\mu_1\|$ therefore gives
$$\nu(\Omega) = 0.$$

But $\nu$ is a **positive** measure. A positive measure with zero total mass is identically zero: for every Borel set $E \subseteq \Omega$, we have $0 \le \nu(E) \le \nu(\Omega) = 0$, so $\nu(E) = 0$.

Hence $\nu = 0$, which means $\mu_2 - \mu_1 = 0$, i.e.,
$$\mu_1 = \mu_2. \qquad \blacksquare$$

